messages:
anna: Heads-up: QA hit a weird streaming glitch hitting the public edge + auth-refresh flow. When the proxy rewrites bodies (adds small comment lines) we occasionally see token reordering or stray empty tokens in the browser quickstart. Filing notes here to coordinate a fix.

ben: Can you paste a minimal raw capture? Which SDK/runtime?

anna: Repro in browser quickstart (chromium). Sequence we saw (simplified):

```
server chunk1 -> data: {\"t\":\"A\"}

server chunk2 -> data: {\"t\":\"B_part1\"}
# proxy inserts comment here -> :proxy-note

server chunk3 -> data: {\"t\":\"B_part2\"}
server chunk4 -> data: [DONE]

```

Observed client-side delivered tokens: [\"A\",\"\",\"B\"] or sometimes [\"B\",\"A\"]. The SW/proxy insertion seems to break chunk atomicity and combined with an auth refresh (mid-stream reconnect) the fetch helper can end up reordering.

marta: Hmm. Is your browser helper doing per-chunk decode + split on "\\n\\n"? If so that pattern is fragile when transforms re-chunk or insert lines.

anna: Yes, the original helper did: read -> decoder.decode(chunk, {stream:true}) -> split on "\\n\\n". Works most of the time but fails with injected comments and immediate reconnects.

ben: Two probable causes: 1) service/proxy injects a comment that lands between bytes of a multi-line event so splitting logic mis-attributes boundaries, 2) auth-refresh reconnect swaps readers non-atomically leading to interleaving of leftover buffer + new stream. We saw similar in server-side SDK bugs.

marta: Proposed fixes: (a) switch the helper to a byte-buffered assembly using TextDecoder streaming mode and only split on completed separators in the decoded text, (b) during reconnect, pause consumer, drain and process leftover bytes treating cancellation as explicit boundary, then swap readers atomically. Also drop no-op empty-data events.

anna: I sketched a small helper change (pseudo):

```js
// keep bytes until we see \\n\\n in decoded text
let byteBuf = new Uint8Array(0);
const td = new TextDecoder('utf-8');
for await (const chunk of reader) {
  byteBuf = concat(byteBuf, chunk);
  const text = td.decode(byteBuf, {stream: true});
  const parts = text.split('\\n\\n');
  // the last part might be incomplete -> re-encode to byteBuf for next loop
  emitComplete(parts.slice(0, -1));
  byteBuf = encode(parts[parts.length - 1], 'utf-8');
}
// on done: treat leftover as final boundary and emit
```

ben: That handles multibyte splits and comment insertions better. Also add a small guard to bound the byteBuf (e.g., 256KB) and log when it's growing too large.

marta: For the reconnect swap: atomic handoff is important. Proposed state machine: 1) set reconnecting flag, 2) pause event consumer loop, 3) drain/process leftover bytes as final events, 4) attach new reader and clear reconnecting flag, 5) resume. That avoids two producers writing to the same event queue concurrently.

infra-bot: Issue #8122 created: web-sdk: resilient fetch streaming helper for proxy-rechunking + auth-refresh; add byte-buffer parser + atomic reconnect swap. Assigned to @marta.

anna: I opened PR #8402 with tests: Playwright e2e that registers a tiny proxy-style transform (injects :proxy-note and randomly splits chunks), plus unit tests simulating split multibyte frames and comment-in-middle cases. Also added a debug flag --sse-raw to dump raw bytes for debugging.

qa: Ran the e2e in staging with 5k randomized trials against the proxy transform. Before the patch we saw ~1.8% trials with reorder/empty token; after the patch it's down to 0% in the run. Looks good. :100:

ben: Nice. Two follow-ups: (1) add a cookbook note advising integrators about SW/proxy transforms and recommending SDK helper usage, (2) add a small migration note in CHANGELOG about the new --sse-raw flag.

marta: Will do both. Also adding a short paragraph in the quickstart about graceful idempotent retries: clients should dedupe final tokens when a reconnect occurs soon after a [DONE].

anna: Thanks team. Marking this resolved for now; we'll keep an eye on field telemetry. If anyone sees similar artifacts in other runtimes ping this thread. :pray:
